Task seon.render.value/throwable-safe-memoize-retains-classes. seon.render.value/throwable-safe (render/value.clj:424) is clojure.core/memoize keyed by Class: unbounded, hand-rolled, never evicted. Every Throwable class it sees stays strongly reachable, and with it the DynamicClassLoader that defined it (an agent's defrecord or ex subclass evaluated in SCI or the JVM REPL). The idle heap showed 11,867 class loaders (heap-findings 2026-09-26), cause not yet attributed. Bound it as a cache kind keyed weakly by class, or compute it per call (four getMethod lookups).

#{"docs/research/agent-platform/cache-central-design-2026-09-26.md" "tmp/orchestrator/heap-findings-2026-09-26.md"}

Task seon.schema/projection-memos-stampede-and-swap-on-every-hit. seon.schema/build-projection (schema.clj:2344), seon.schema/declaration-projection (:1326) and seon.schema.edn/packaged-population (edn.clj:385) call clojure.core.cache.wrapped/lookup-or-miss with an undelayed derivation. That function builds one delay per CALLER, not per key (core.cache 1.1.234 wrapped.clj), so N concurrent misses of one key derive N times: measured 8 derivations for 8 concurrent misses (tmp/cache-central-design-opus/bench.clj), at about 390 ms and 570 MB allocated per projection build. It also swap!s the atom on every hit: 400-860 ns per hit single-threaded and 760-1,800 ns under 8 threads, against 14-23 ns for cache/lookup. Derive once per key (a stored delay, as seon.db/arity-base already does) and read hits without a swap.

#{"docs/research/agent-platform/cache-central-design-2026-09-26.md"}

Task my.program/write-branch-refuses-a-file-a-sibling-change-already-landed-in. Fan-out 9b (default f54e73c83, 2026-10-09) landed `seon.ref/span-candidate` in 849ad0a2a; the next agent in the same file, fan9b-reply, changed only `seon.ref/section-error` on a branch of main, whose analysis of src/seon/ref.clj is still the pre-849ad0a2a file. `(my.program/write-branch! {:seon.fn/root "/Users/sean/src/seon"})` refused with "The file changed after its declarations were analyzed." (155 ms / 236 MB), so two agents' changes to different declarations of one file cannot both write back between restarts; root wrote into a copy of the base file and applied the diff by hand. Done when write-back stages an exact-span change onto a file whose other declarations changed since the branch's base (refusing only when the changed declaration's own span moved or differs), proven by writing back a branch's change to one declaration after another declaration of that file was rewritten.

Task seon.cluster.agent/a-trivial-form-waits-on-two-durable-commits. A one-form MCP run costs 155 ms warm in the JVM (168 ms through the bridge), 128 ms of it two durable Datahike commits (submission in submit-source!, settlement in settle-batch!), 64 ms each; evaluation is 1-2 ms. Under the ruled durability (sync-blob? and commit-graph? false inadmissible) a commit on this workstation cannot go below ~30 ms of forces, so a single-digit-ms form needs the owner to choose: keep two durable commits and trim (~115 ms), make the submission a non-durable hand-off so one commit per form remains (~60 ms), or relax durability for evaluation records. Options priced in the landing.

#{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/perf-eval-overhead-2026-09-26.md"}

Task seon.cluster.agent/reply-size-is-measured-by-printing-the-sources. submit-source-in-projection records `:seon.turn/reply-size` as (count (pr-str sources)): it prints the segmented sources to count characters, a printer use outside the value printer on every MCP SCI evaluation. Correct: the size is the submitted text's length (the reply it stands for), computed without printing.

#{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/plan-research/r-read-notes.md"}

Task seon.cluster/record-jvm-evaluations-on-shared-path. A JVM-mode MCP evaluation is evaluated by the prepl and projected by mcp-project, but never recorded: it has no evaluation record, no settlement window and so no fault observation, unlike an SCI run. The JVM evaluator should be a stage value supplied to the one read-evaluate-record-print path (no custody), so its value, its error in the one shape and its window's faults are the same saved observation MCP and the context show.

#{"docs/research/agent-platform/error-delivery-audit-2026-09-26.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/landing/err-s4-observation-opus-2026-09-26.md"}

Task seon.db/custody-scopes-widen. Held custody is not a positive value: call-with-custody rebinds *conn* to any connection, including one conjured by branch-connection, `seon.cluster.boot/connection` or `seon.cluster.registry/active-branch-connection`, all reachable from agent SCI. A branch agent read :cluster-default and its 23 agents; foreign-connection-error then compares the cluster with itself; call-without-custody clears *conn* so agent code looks like a JVM REPL; the db.clj:325 refusal teaches agents (`seon.cluster.boot/connection` 'default'); capability handlers such as `seon.shell.jvm/run` run without admission. Violates 'Authority comes only from held custody'. Needs the owner's delivery ruling (options: refuse delivery out of a branch, declared deliveries at the writer, or root pulling).

#{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/fix-dogfood-api-2026-09-26.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/landing/custody-authority-2026-09-26.md"}

Task seon.fn/call-reach-refuses-every-gate. Every armed test selection refuses. call-reach hands identity-names a set of caller entities (seed-callers and reaching are sets), and identity-names declares [:sequential :int], so gate-set, the map arity of gate-sets, tests-reaching and reverse-closure all answer 'seon.fn/identity-names :malli.core/invalid-input: the refusal could not be built.' Measured at HEAD 013e12924 after the nuke: (`seon.fn/gate-set` db 'my.plan/current) refuses in 8.8 ms, and a fully contracted (defn tlo-inc ...) on a fresh MCP branch refuses the same way (error signature 3ee1d7a90eaf). No agent can install a contracted function, and `my.test/check` and the install gate cannot select tests.

#{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/inv-test-selection-cost-opus-2026-09-26.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/landing/task-log-2026-09-26.md"}

Task seon.operator/a-test-the-candidate-adds-is-not-run-by-the-check. `seon.operator/check!` never runs a deftest the candidate adds: lane ref-context's candidate (HEAD fa92a2503 plus `test/seon/ref_test.clj` with two new deftests, 2026-10-09) ran `--test seon.ref-test` as executed 14 of the file's 16 deftests, and naming a new one by var (`--test seon.ref-test/a-referenced-doc-shows-its-heading-size-and-one-read`) refuses 'The requested test is not eligible.' (`seon.test/run`, :seon.test/identity-unresolved), while an existing var in the same file runs. So a slice's own new proof is never proven by the check and a namespace run reports green without it. Done when the candidate's new deftests are adopted as tests and run under a namespace or var request.

Task seon.profile/scope-wrappers-report-callback-time-as-work. Higher-order scope wrappers are armed with ordinary profile cells, so their inclusive time is their callback's, waits included. `seon.flow/graph-executor` (flow.clj:96) runs every Flow proc thread's whole life inside `seon.schema/call-with-projection-state`: the previous default reported it 5,223,273 ms total, max 754,816 ms, x269, which is proc threads blocked on channels, not work. call-with-projection (x51-x487), call-with-forms and `seon.db/call-with-custody` rank the same way and lead every over-one-second list. A reader of by-total/over-second takes them for costs. Rank self-evident work: omit or mark functions whose declared input includes a callback, or report the callee instead.

#{"docs/research/agent-platform/perf-audit-2026-09-27.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/stability-sweep-2026-09-27.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/landing/perf-boot-opus-2026-09-26.md"}

Task seon.program/a-docstring-edit-changes-the-definition-digest. definition-digest includes the docstring and the source text carrying it, so a docstring-only edit selects its callers' tests (observed on my.note/add!, since deleted: its doc edit gated 4 unrelated tests) and invalidates reuse through the reach digest's leaf. Owner ruling: docstring edits select nothing. Recommended option 1: an executable digest derived from the entity without doc and with the source read without its docstring (~40 + 15 lines).

#{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/inv-test-selection-cost-opus-2026-09-26.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/dogfood-maintainer-opus-2026-09-26.md"}

Task seon.ref/a-referenced-doc-path-reads-as-unresolved. `seon.ref/references` answers every based path in a task intent (a backticked `AGENTS.md#data-and-contracts`, `reference-code/malli/README.md#quickstart`, or a relative Markdown link) as `:problem-kind :unresolved` although `seon.ref/section` finds it, so a generated `seon.contracts/task` names its guide and the agent's block calls it broken (card `docs/seon/schedule/cards/CS-01c3.md`). Done when a path that exists resolves to `:seon.ref/heading`, `:seon.ref/tokens`, its `:seon.ref/pin` under `reference-code/` and the one read `(my.fs/section reference)`, never its text; a missing file stays `:unresolved` and a missing heading reads `:anchor-missing`.

Task seon.ref/a-referenced-function-shows-no-callers-or-tests. `seon.ref/references` resolves a backticked function in a task intent to its arglists, spec, doc summary and the operations `(doc f)` and `(my.program/source 'f)` only (lane contract-gen, 2026-10-09, `seon.fs/attributes` in a generated `seon.contracts/task`, about 211 tokens for two names). A contract task needs the function's callers and reaching tests to choose a schema, and the generated intent copies nothing, so the agent spends calls finding them. Done when a resolved function's view offers its callers and reaching tests as operations (`my.program/callers`, `seon.fn/gating-tests`), asserted on the view's data.

Task seon.sci.eval/a-test-namespace-is-empty-in-an-agents-context. An agent cannot define a deftest that uses its test file's helpers. In default (fa92a2503, 2026-10-09) every test namespace and `seon.test-support` exist in an agent's SCI context as empty namespaces (no publics, no aliases): the JVM had not loaded them when `seon.sci.eval/build-base-ctx` ran, `in-ns` answers the empty one and `(require '[seon.test-support :as support])` answers nil with nothing bound, even after the JVM loads it, until `:reload` (`seon.sci.eval/load-host-namespace` is never asked). Lane repl-only-a5 needed a JVM-mode require of the committed test namespace, `require ... :reload`, a redefinition of the file's private helper and a placeholder deftest before its proof could be admitted; the admitted test then answered "The admitted identity has no executable test Var." in the branch test step. Done when an agent's first `(in-ns 'my.program-test)` resolves the file's aliases and helpers and a deftest it defines there runs in the branch test step.

Task seon.sci.eval/agent-form-writes-name-an-unwritten-evaluation. On live e405f51be (one-commit A) every write an agent form makes on its branch refuses 'Nothing found for entity id [`:seon.cluster.eval/id` <this form>]': the evaluation's receipt lookup ref (`seon.sci.eval/evaluate`, receipt at eval.clj:4341-4343) names the evaluation entity that is written only at settlement. It breaks (`seon.db/transact!` ...) at the REPL and every test run from the agent REPL (`my.test/run-owned` admission), so no agent can prove a change. Reproduced 2026-09-26 20:38Z and 20:46Z on branch merge-cut1-opus (evals 78f381684a44, 82cf53bace3b). Still live on 7c56fb226 default (2026-10-08, lane fs-root): (`seon.config/apply!` {`:seon.boot/cluster-name` "default" `:seon.config/manifest` {`:seon.config.fs/working-root` "/Users/sean/src/seon"}}) from agent fs-root's SCI context refused the same way in 385 ms and 38,412 armed calls; the same apply in JVM mode with the branch connection passed explicitly committed in 244 ms.

#{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/merge-cut1-opus-2026-09-26.md"}

Task seon.test.runner/drift-prints-unbounded. run-vars! appends (pr-str drift) to the failure message (runner.clj:435): one concurrent-run failure message measured 14,262 characters naming 486 Vars. Nothing prints outside the floor. Done when the drift reaches the result as data bounded once by the floor (`seon.render.value/bounded-text`), and a regression with a large drift asserts the bound.

#{"docs/research/agent-platform/test-agents-concurrency-2026-09-27.md"}

Task seon.test.runner/worker-drift-prints-its-sets-unbounded. The in-process runner appends "Worker-global state changed: " (pr-str drift) to a result's failure message (runner.clj, in-process result capture): drift holds the whole native sets of changed process-global members (272a02eab), printed outside the floor with no bound. The failure-report path already renders through report-value under the value renderer's bound (failure-report); drift must too. A 2 GB unbounded print of a failure :actual killed default on 2026-09-28T00:07Z (pid 42397, a lane's probe script); the same pattern here is one large drift away. Render drift with `seon.render.value/bounded-text` or keep it as data on the result and print it only through the floor.

#{"src/seon/test/runner.clj"}

Task seon.test/a-test-result-keeps-no-cost. `seon.test/run` measures each member's body (`seon.test.timing/run-ms`) and then discards the cost: the stored member keeps counts and `:seon.test.member/reach-digest`, so no later comparison can see a branch that made a reaching test do 1,000x the work. Each completed member stores `:seon.test.member/calls` (its own `seon.batch/sink`), `:seon.test.member/alloc-bytes` (its body thread), `:seon.test.member/run-ms` and at most `:seon.config.test.cost/top` `:seon.test.member/top` rows, written by `seon.test.runner/complete-members`.

#{"docs/seon/schedule/cards/COST-REG.md"}

Task seon.turn/a-host-bound-redefinition-runs-its-reaching-tests-in-sci. Defining the host-bound `seon.sci.eval/build-base-ctx` on a branch (lane repl-only-a5, default fa92a2503, 2026-10-09) made the eval request's test step run 115 reaching tests (10.5 s of an 11.1 s request), 13 red and 10 errored, a reply of about 3,000 tokens, although a host-bound change runs compiled until merge and only `my.test/check-platform` can prove it; the heap's live set rose from 0.66 GB to 3.17 GB over this and the next minutes, after which every evaluation was refused for memory pressure. Done when the test step answers a host-bound change by naming its platform proof instead of running reaching tests in SCI.

Task seon.turn/analyze-settlement-is-unreached. seon.turn/analyze-settlement re-analyzes a settled program entity outside SCI, so it cannot see declared-without-root Vars and drops their call edges (the gap b3f1a7ae7 fixed in sci.eval/analyzed-rows). It appears unreached in production: every settle! call site passes :seon.error/value, ordinary runs settle through receipt-settle-batch-tx which does not re-analyze, and its only database-arity caller with a program entity is test/seon/fn_test.clj:544. Verify no caller, then delete it and that test (fix-declare-edges, b3f1a7ae7).

#{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/program-test-candidate-projection-2026-09-27.md"}

Task seon.turn/the-unbound-check-walks-the-live-value-without-a-bound. unbound-value? walks the evaluation's live value with (tree-seq coll? seq value) after disarm: no bound, no deadline, no interrupt, and it ran twice per form (in the since-deleted problem routing and in evaluation-facts). An evaluation returning (range) realized 115.9 M elements (10.2 GB, 93.7% of heap) and killed default pid 63809 with OutOfMemoryError; a finite (map f (range 200000)) is realized in full although 32 are shown. Answer 'contains SCI's unbound marker' from admission's one bounded walk inside the arm (its object-node leaves) and delete the live-value walk at both callers. Class regression: an ordinary run of (range) settles and old gen stays flat.

#{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/oom-63809-2026-09-27.md"}

Task seon.task/assignment-requires-an-observed-failing-proof. `seon.task/assign!` assigned a task when its proof run errored (attaching a proof-run error) and when the run held no result for a proof, so unknown proof state became work (astra day audit finding 7). Done when assignment requires a proof the run in the agent's world observed failing: a run that errored refuses `:proof-run-failed` with the error beside it (infrastructure, never work), proofs that all pass refuse `:proofs-green`, and a run observing none failing refuses `:proofs-unobserved`; `:seon.task/refused` names each in `:seon.task/refused`.

#{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-10/astra-day-audit.md"}

Task seon.task/assignment-runs-its-proofs-in-the-agents-world. `seon.task/assign!` runs the task's proofs at assignment in exactly the environment the agent will use: its branch and SCI world, acquired through the cluster handle (`:seon.agent/context-source`, supplied from the world) by `seon.cluster.agent/acquire-context!` as its eval requests acquire it, with the runner (`seon.test/run`) and the bound (`seon.turn/step-limit-ms`) its automatic test step uses. The results are recorded on the agent's branch as ordinary test evidence, so the agent's first block (`seon.context/proofs`) shows each proof red with its failing data, and the all-green refusal is decided from this run alone, never from older evidence (`seon.test/verified?`). Today assign! reads `seon.test/verified?` at main and runs nothing: the block shows the proof pending, and an agent's first run of it is the first evidence.

Task my.program/a-refused-native-call-answers-as-a-success-value. (remove-ns 'audit.gone) in an agent's SCI run answers the refusal map as the call's VALUE (MCP isError false) and leaves the namespace in place ((find-ns 'audit.gone) answers it), even for a namespace the same run created; plain SCI removes it. seon.sci.eval/native-mutation returns (unreduced refusal) from my.program/native-call-refusal instead of throwing, and remove-ns/ns-unalias are refused whenever a connection exists. The owner's law (2026-10-08): any valid Clojure SCI executes works serially. Correct: remove-ns removes the namespace and records the removal through the write observer (it already observes :remove-ns); see my.program/native-calls-are-refused-instead-of-routed. Proof: a deftest where remove-ns of an agent namespace answers nil and the namespace entity is retracted at settlement.

#{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/repl-audit/repl-audit-opus-audit.md"}

Task my.program/native-calls-are-refused-instead-of-routed. native-call-refusal refuses intern and alter-var-root of program Vars, which unindexed-change already refuses (a duplicate check), and refuses remove-ns and ns-unalias with advice to call my.program/remove-ns! instead of calling it. Delete the duplicate arms and route the native forms to the owners (about -25 of 44 lines).

#{"docs/research/agent-platform/agent-restrictions-and-rollback-2026-09-26.md"}

Task my.program/refusal-names-nonexistent-define. native-call-refusal tells agents to use my.program/define!, which does not exist (src/my/program.clj:791); a refusal's fix must name a real entrance.

#{"docs/research/agent-platform/docs-exploration-2026-09-26.md"}

Task seon.flow/a-reply-carries-its-step-cost. A redefinition's `:seon.repl/request` reports about 28 MB of a 1,150 to 2,100 MB window, because `seon.batch/request-cost` counts evaluation arms only. Done when `seon.flow/var-process` brackets the agent's turn step with its platform thread's allocated bytes and CPU time and the reply carries them as `:seon.cost/step` (`:seon.cost/alloc-mb`, `:seon.cost/cpu-ms`), read by `seon.turn/step` when it builds the reply, so the attributed share of `seon.batch/jvm-window` reaches the crude 60% the proof asks for (stage 12 raises it to 90%); the labels `:seon.cost/arm-scope` and `:seon.cost/causal-attribution` go in the same change. Chain stage 2 of card FEEDBACK-LOOP.

#{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-10/measurement.md" "docs/seon/schedule/cards/FEEDBACK-LOOP.md#6. Slicing: stages of about fifteen minutes"}

Task seon.profile/cost-entities-hold-the-latest-values. Per-function cost lives in three places nobody reads: `:seon.db.transaction/{ms,calls,hot,repeated}` stamps (no reader), `:seon.profile.measurement/*` bucket entities (read only by a comparison being deleted) and live cells that reset at restart. Done when `seon.profile/transaction-observation` returns, for the world's sink, upserts of the `:seon.fn.cost/entity` of each definition with at least 20 calls whose stored per-call value is absent, whose digest differs, or whose self time moved by half a log2 bucket (a new `moved?` rule, no new dial), `seon.db/stamp-transaction` adds them to the transaction the world already writes (zero extra transactions, at most definitions defined + tests run + `:seon.config.test.cost/top` entities per request), every value attribute is declared without history (a replaced value leaves one datom), and the four `:seon.db.transaction` stamps and their producer are deleted. Supersedes `seon.profile/transaction-stamps-store-hot-and-repeated-sets-nothing-reads`. Chain stage 5 of card FEEDBACK-LOOP.

#{"docs/seon/schedule/cards/FEEDBACK-LOOP.md#3. The data model"}

Task seon.profile/transaction-stamps-store-hot-and-repeated-sets-nothing-reads. `seon.profile/transaction-observation` stamps every transaction over its call/ms threshold with `:seon.db.transaction/hot` (up to 20 [sym digest calls ms] tuples) and `:seon.db.transaction/repeated`; `seon.db/stamp-transaction` attaches them. No src reader exists (only seon.profile-test asserts them). A 17-test run-owned on a branch agent (default, 2026-10-07, db-watch lane) stamped 20+40 hot/repeated datoms (~6 KB of values) on its record-tx and 28 (~2.8 KB) on its request tx. Every stored fact names its consumer or is deleted: delete both attributes and their derivation (the explanation of a slow operation reads the live profile, not stored stamps), or name the consumer. Done when no transaction carries them and the per-test-run datom count (watch.clj :since-basis) drops accordingly.

#{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/db-watch/findings.md"}

